  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 215 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nBy Sunday, a strong low over Hudson Bay will dislodge high pressure \nfrom the Great Lakes, pushing it eastward. A diffuse frontal \nboundary from this system will sag southeastward from the northern \nPlains, eventually reaching the CWA by Monday and bringing a \ncooldown to at least part of the CWA. However, model timing still \nremains quite poor with regard to the front, resulting in a near 10 \ndegree spread across the northwestern half. With this slight cooldown,\nhighs are expected to fall into the mid 60s to mid 70s but \nremain above the normal highs for late October.\n\nLooking ahead to next week, while guidance has a wide spread \nregarding that pattern aloft, an eventual return to a more \nactive pattern is expected. Some models, such as the ECMWF, \nfavor a west CONUS trough and eastern CONUS ridge, while the GFS\nfavors a more shallow, near- zonal flow with embedded waves. \nWhile the pattern aloft remains unclear this far out, a \ncontinuation of our above-normal temperature streak appears \nlikely through the end of October with highs generally in the \n60s to 70s.\n\nAdditionally, while guidance continues to trend drier along the \nboundary on Monday, precipitation chances trend higher toward\nthe end of the week. However, there is plenty of uncertainty on\nplacement and timing of systems this far out and the transition\nto an active pattern may hold off until next weekend. What will\nfavor more widespread precipitation, in addition to a more \nactive pattern, is an increasingly moist airmass with PWATs near\n0.8-1.0 inches, or around the 75-90th percentiles.",
